@startuml WsCacheInvalidation

!include ../_style.puml

title WS-driven cache invalidation across tabs (follow as the worked example)

actor "User A" as user_a
actor "User B" as user_b

box "User A clients" #EEF2F8
participant "Tab 1\n(active)" as tab1
participant "Tab 2\n(open, profile of B)" as tab2
end box

box "User B clients" #EEF2F8
participant "Tab 3" as tab3
end box

box "Backend" #F8EFE5
participant "FollowedView\n(DRF)" as view
database "Database" as db
participant "Channels layer\n(user groups)" as channels
end box

note over tab1, tab3
Each tab is a separate WS consumer in the same user_group(uuid).
A mutation that fans out via WS reaches every tab of every connected
user, so React Query caches stay coherent without per-tab polling.
end note

== Mutation ==
user_a -> tab1 : click "Follow B"
tab1 -> view : POST /users/{B}/followed
view -> db : insert UserFollow(A, B)
view -> db : insert FollowerNotification(A -> B)

== Broadcast ==
view -> channels : send_ws_event(user_group(A), "user.followed", {from, to})
view -> channels : send_ws_event(user_group(B), "user.followed", {from, to})
view --> tab1 : 204 No Content

channels -->> tab1 : ws event "user.followed"
channels -->> tab2 : ws event "user.followed"
channels -->> tab3 : ws event "user.followed"

== Cache invalidation (per tab) ==
note over tab1, tab3
useUserWsEvents.ts (frontend) reacts identically in every tab:
invalidates userKeys.followed(from_uuid), userKeys.friends(from_uuid),
userKeys.followers(to_uuid). TanStack refetches only the queries that
are currently mounted in that tab.
end note

tab1 -> view : refetch GET /users/{A}/followed (mounted)
tab2 -> view : refetch GET /users/{B}/followers (mounted on B's profile)
tab3 -> view : refetch GET /users/{B}/followers (if mounted)

user_b -> tab3 : sees new follower without page reload

== Side note: same pattern, other features ==
note over channels, tab3
The same invalidate-on-WS pattern is reused by:
  * notifications.changed -> notificationKeys.base
  * user.library.changed / user.liked_songs.changed
    / user.liked_collections.changed -> userKeys.likedSongs etc.
  * queue.changed -> queueKeys.base
  * chat.messages.changed -> publicationKeys.chatMessages(uuid)
  * collection.comments.changed -> publicationKeys.comments(uuid)
Only the event name and the invalidated keys differ.
end note

@enduml
